davisd@auburn.ee.washington.edu writes:
> 
> Ray writes:
> 
> >    Old age is a disease of our cell machinery
> > and super-diets aren't going to extend their life to 300 years. They
> > will break down over time and need to be repaired. I'd like you
> > to demonstrate otherwise.
> 
> I'd like you to demonstrate your claims first. Our bodies constantly
> repair themselves. Your claim that they cannot do so indefinitely
> (more precisely, for 300 years) without nanotech looks like pure
> conjecture. If you can back it up, do so, but you've got quite a job
> before you. Proving something impossible is much more work than
> proving something possible.

  If our bodies were so good at repairing themselves we wouldn't be
discussing this. The fact is, they aren't. Not all errors get corrected
and they accumulate with time. Some experiments have suggested that
there is a "clock" in cells which limits the number of possible times
they will divide thus limiting replacement and renewal. And what
about the brain? Brain cells can and do die, but they don't replace
themselves.

  Finally, if you plot lifespan vs time you see that the average lifespan
has been getting longer but that it seems to be tapering off a little
over 120. As you get older, more and more things in the body have
a higher probability of failure as if the whole system was failing
at once and not particular subsystems. Thus, even if you survive
cancer and heart disease with a good diet, you will die from other more
rare diseases.

  I certainly can't prove this, you can't prove a negative, but there 
certainly is evidence for my position, namely that no recorded human
has lived to 300, not even close.  Of course it's conjecture, it's all
conjecture. The science of aging isn't that advanced, but one can make
reasonably strong conjectures based on available evidence. 

  I'd like you to prove that human cell machinery will completely
repair itself WITHOUT error accumulation. It seems much more likely
that some errors will miss detection and over time, that will build
to danger levels. It's a completely reasonable position to hold.

  IMHO, super diets are a good bet to increase your chances of living
long enough to see good artificial cell repair and/or cybernetics/cryonics.
Don't count on them giving you immortality or any reasonable approximation.
That will require post-biological technology.

(there are also reasonable evolutionary arguments for why our cell machinery
isn't programmed for extremely long lifespans. See Engines of Creation)


