RE: Bill Garland <bill@kean.ucs.mun.ca> X-Message-Number: #94-1-1486

Some brief reactions and penny's worth:
>
>My second question about a startup involves a question of extropian
>morality. 

I'd be more concerned about making your business a success. Play by the
rules, but play them to your maximum advantage. (I.e., learn the rules; tax
attorneys help.) As often as not, the government is the friend of business,
irrespective of appearances.


>Meanwhile, in my attempt to rise out of a condition that seemed to
>be everlasting, namely chronic poverty, 

Might be time to study a bit more about business, how it operates, and seek
out some successful people to mentor you. Success breeds success; failure
breeds failure. Poverty implies the latter. It might be worth focusing on
skills more than cash for the time being, until poverty is pushed out of
the active vocabulary. Poverty and sharp business skills are not likely to
co-occur without extreme extenuating circumstances.


>Anyway, I have written a traditional-style Business Plan, for my
>computer services company, and wrote down some of my ideas for
...
>rewrite, he figures it would be fairly easy for me to walk into
>some government offices and walk out with about $250,000 of 
>money extorted from hapless taxpayers (my new favourite phrase).

Whether you're seeking $1/4mil or any other amount, the key considerations
are two: (1) sources and uses; and (2) likelihood of ROI to the investor.
Is it worth taking any money if it's to be a failure? Perhaps a good bet
would be to seek some scholarship money to study business courses, or go
work for a few startups to get a feel for the challenges they face. Track
record is important: if you work for a failing enterprise it's not a black
mark; if you are the founder of one, the stigma lingers. Bankruptcy lingers
very long. Tim May has made a key point eloquently elsewhere:  learn your
hard lessons on someone else's time and money -- I'd add, within someone
else's organization.  The main causal factors in poverty are revealed in a
mirror or through an honest attitude check.

>
>But if there is such free money available, shouldn't I take it?

It's a question if this money could help. The posting doesn't appear to
offer clear "sources/uses" for the cash.  Why do anything in business
without a clear purpose? Will it actually help you develop a business, or
just repeat earlier difficulties?  It seems the most important thing here
is to learn how to do business and overcome poverty. 

If I had a chance to get $250,000 you bet I'd think hard about it. But
depending on the string attached, and the records the disbursers might
keep, my behavior would be moderated accordingly. 

>
>My competitors would certainly take it. If they can use this
>money to go after my market, they will out-compete me and win
>the Customers' dollars. 

This appears to be a conundrum. If you're up to the level of challenge your
competitors' can muster, then this is a good point. Grab the cash and go
after the weaker ones to put them out of your misery immediately. If you're
not, then it's a losing proposition to begin with and a better path might
well be to acquire some basic business training. In business, it's wise to
place your bets and efforts where the likelihood of success is greatest.

Plus, service businesses require an emphasis on personality and face to
face contact in the start-up phases, especially those that deal with the
public. Make a good personal inventory to see if this fits your bill. Many
service companies fail because the personalities of the founders just don't
fit the necessities of the market -- mucho flesh pressing, pitching and
catering to client's needs. Once you get rolling, you can hire people for
this, but at the beginning it falls to the entrepreneur. If you're not
already a somewhat gregarious individual who is comfortable with strangers
and touting the solutions your ways provide, it might be wise to deeply
consider putting off a service business unless you're confident of your
razor-sharp business skills (typically measured in terms of net worth and
cash on hand). Business is a game, but a very serious one that it's wise to
play to win. The penalities often outstrip the rewards to the careless.

Lastly, I'm, not one to speak about somebody else's morality.

