Date:     Wed, 5 Jan 94 13:43:50 GMT
From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Open Books

I've heard that the dismal success rate for new businesses can be
inverted if the business keeps open books--lets anyone see its
financial records. I've also heard that you can get almost as good
results if you don't let anyone else look at your records, but *do*
look at them yourself.

There was an article in the 12/20 Wall Street Journal about
Springfield ReManufacturing Corp going from the edge of bankruptcy
to rapid growth as a result of open books (at least to its 
employees) and a generous stock-sharing plan. It turns out that
employees will make an effort to help a company do well if they
are rewarded for it and have the information they need.

Nancy Lebovitz


